    "lawCode": "Amd §206, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8315\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to review of policies\nand practices relating to any infectious disease outbreak in correction-\nal facilities, including the treatment and prevention of the disease\namong inmates and staff\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL::\n\nTo expand Department of Health (DOH) review of health policies and prac-\ntices in Department of Corrections and Community Supervision (DOCCS) and\nlocal correctional facilities in the event of infectious disease\noutbreaks.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS::\n\nSection one amends subdivision 26 of Public Health Law § 206 by adding\ninfectious disease outbreaks to the situations in which DOH may review\npolicies and practices of DOCCS and local jail facilities.\n\nSection one also requires monitoring of availability of personal protec-\ntive equipment; ensuring that inmates are not inappropriately confined;\nand directs the commissioner to make recommendations regarding early\nrelease; medical parole; or transfers of inmates to medical facilities\nas needed.\n\nSection two: effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION::\n\nNew York has over 40,000 inmates in DOCCS custody and another 25,000 in\nlocal correctional facilities. These inmates rely on correctional health\ncare providers for their medical care. Many inmates are at high risk for\nillnesses related to poverty, addiction or mental illness, and in the\nevent of infectious disease outbreaks the inability to maintain personal\ndistance and lack of access to sanitary supplies are especially danger-\nous.\n\nSubdivision 26 of PHL § 206, enacted in 2009, authorized DoH to review\npolicies and practices in DOCCS and local facilities relating to\nHIV/AIDS and Hepatitis C. Implementation includes reviews of HIV and\nHepatitis C services in DOCCS facilities done by State contractor IPRO,\nincluding on-site reviews, staff interviews, and medical record review.\nThe 2009 law has been successful in addressing HIV/AIDS with a viral\nsuppression rate consistently over 90% in DOCCS, and ten times more\nindividuals currently receive Hepatitis C care in NYS DOCCS than any\nother state.\n\nAdvocates have urged the expansion of this successful law. Last year the\nGovernor vetoed a bill that would've expanded it to additional inmate\ncategories despite calling it \"laudable\" in his veto message.  COVID-19\nhas demonstrated the particular risks of infectious disease outbreaks in\nprisons and jails. As of April, rates of positive tests in correctional\nsettings were even higher than in the New York City general public,\nthough the number of tests performed in prisons and jails is unaccepta-\nbly low.(1) COVID-19 spread widely among both inmates and staff alike,\nand it took legal action for the City of New York to provide masks to\ncorrections officers.(2) Advocates have also called for expansion of\nmedical parole in order to reduce the spread. This bill gives DOH the\nsame authority to review DOCCS and local jail policies and practices in\nrelation to infectious disease outbreaks, including ongoing reporting,\nas they currently have under the 2009 law regarding HIV/AIDS and Hepati-\ntis C services.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY::\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS::\n\nMinimal.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE::  Immediate.\n\n(1)https://thecity.nyc/2020/04/new-york- prison-virus-cases-mounting-as-\nstate-city- plateau.html\n\n(2)https://nypost.com/2020/04/04/nyc- must-give-coronavirus-gear-to-con-\nections- officers-judge/\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
